{"the_previous_limitation": "The previous limitation refers to the drawback of global metrics in semantic segmentation, where they fail to adequately capture the performance differences across classes with varying relevance and frequency of appearance. This limitation is particularly pronounced in applications where certain classes are more critical despite having fewer pixels."},
{"classwise_metrics__mean__that_we_cannot_compare_different_segmentation_solutions_directly": "The mean, in the context of classwise metrics, refers to the average value of the chosen class-wise metric across all classes. This averaging helps to mitigate the effects of imbalanced class frequencies and provides a more balanced comparison between different segmentation solutions."},
{"that_we_cannot_compare_different_segmentation_solutions_directly": "The statement \"that we cannot compare different segmentation solutions directly\" highlights a limitation of using classwise metrics. Since classwise metrics calculate accuracy for each class individually, without specifying the quantitative relevance of each class, it becomes challenging to directly compare the overall performance of different segmentation solutions."},
{"alternative_metric": "The alternative metric refers to a method used in evaluating segmentation approaches, particularly when dealing with imbalanced class frequencies in semantic segmentation tasks. It aims to provide a more balanced assessment compared to global metrics."},
