{"average_the_chosen_class_wise_metric_according_to_the_total_number_of_classes": "Averaging the chosen class-wise metric according to the total number of classes is a technique used to mitigate the effects of imbalanced class frequencies. This approach calculates a class-wise average metric, which is less influenced by the frequency of appearance of each class, thereby providing a more equitable comparison between different segmentation solutions."},
{"class_wise_average": "The class-wise average is a method of evaluating segmentation approaches by averaging the chosen class-wise metric across all classes. This approach aims to provide a more balanced assessment of segmentation performance by considering each class individually.|Here is another description: The class-wise average is a metric used in evaluating segmentation approaches, calculated by averaging the chosen class-wise metric across all classes. It is designed to be less affected by imbalanced class frequencies compared to global metrics."},
{"class_wise_average__is_less_affected_by__imbalanced_class_frequencies_than_global_metrics": "The phrase \"is less affected by\" indicates a comparative relationship where the subject (class-wise average) experiences a lesser degree of influence or impact from a specified factor (imbalanced class frequencies) compared to another method or metric (global metrics)."},
{"imbalanced_class_frequencies_than_global_metrics": "Imbalanced class frequencies refer to a situation in semantic segmentation where certain classes appear much more frequently than others. This imbalance can skew global metrics, making them less reliable indicators of overall segmentation performance. In contrast, class-wise averages are designed to mitigate this issue by providing a more equitable evaluation across all classes."},
