{"when_class_frequencies_are_unbalanced": "The condition 'when class frequencies are unbalanced' describes a scenario where the distribution of classes in the dataset is skewed, meaning some classes have significantly more instances than others. This imbalance can lead to biased evaluation results if global accuracy metrics are used."},
{"class_frequencies": "Class frequencies refer to the distribution of instances across different classes within a dataset. In many practical applications, these frequencies can vary significantly, leading to some classes having many more instances than others."},
{"class_frequencies__are_unbalanced__in_most_scenarios_both_in_real_indoor_and_outdoor_scenes": "The predicate \"are unbalanced\" describes a situation where the distribution of class frequencies is not equal or uniform. This imbalance can skew the performance metrics of machine learning models, making them less reliable for classes with fewer instances."},
{"in_most_scenarios_both_in_real_indoor_and_outdoor_scenes": "In most scenarios both in real indoor and outdoor scenes, class frequencies are typically unbalanced. This means that certain classes may dominate the dataset, while others are underrepresented, which can affect the accuracy and fairness of model predictions in these environments."},
{"global_accuracy_metrics__are_biased_by__the_dominant_classes": "The phrase \"are biased by\" indicates that the global accuracy metrics are influenced or skewed in a particular direction due to certain factors. In this context, it means that the metrics do not provide a fair or accurate representation of model performance because of the uneven distribution of classes."},
